      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2018/02/16/commerce-department-suggests-trump-impose-steep-tariffs-or-quotas-on-foreign-steel-and-aluminum--sources.html",
            "title": "Commerce Department calls for Trump to impose steep tariffs or ...",
            "snippets": [
              "The Commerce Department is recommending that President Trump slap quotas or tariffs on foreign steel and aluminum producers.\nBusiness\nTech\nVideo\nPRO\nEconomy",
              "# Commerce Department calls for Trump to impose steep tariffs or quotas on foreign steel and aluminum\n- The Commerce Department is recommending heavy tariffs and quotas on steel and aluminum imports.\n- The recommendations include a 24 percent global tariff on steel imports, or a charge of at least 53 percent on steel from a dozen countries.\n- Commerce recommended a 7.7 percent tariff on aluminum imports, or a 23.5 percent tariff on aluminum products from China, Hong Kong, Russia, Venezuela and Vietnam.",
              "VIDEO 1:22 01:22\n\nCommerce to unveil options on quotas, tariffs\n\nSquawk Alley\n\nThe Commerce Department on Friday recommended imposing heavy tariffs or quotas on foreign producers of steel and aluminum in the interest of national security, following a trade investigation of imports.\n\nPresident Donald Trump and his administration announced the so-called Section 232 investigation into steel and aluminum imports in April. The investigation sought to determine whether the imports posed a threat to the country's national security",
              "On Friday, Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross reported that steel is in fact important to U.S. national security, and current import flows are adversely impacting the steel industry.\n\n\"[T]he Secretary of Commerce concludes that the present quantities and circumstance of steel imports are 'weakening our internal economy' and threaten to impair the national security as defined in Section 232,\" the department said",
              "In a 262-page document, Ross recommended a couple alternatives for the president to take \"immediate action by adjusting the level of imports through quotas or tariffs.\" The department's goal is to increase demand for American-made metals.\n\nTrump must respond to the reports by April 11 and 19 for steel and aluminum, respectively.",
              "Among the recommendations in the reports are a global tariff of 24 percent on all steel imports",
              "An alternative option would target 12 countries, including China and Brazil, who export the most cheap steal to the U.S. Those countries would be charged new tariffs of 53 percent or higher. In this scenario, countries not listed in the list would see US imports capped at the amount they imported in 2017. A third option would institute a quota on steel imports from all countries, up to 63 percent of what those countries imported in 2017",
              "The aluminum recommendations include a 7.7 percent tariff on imports from all exporter nations. Ross separately suggested a 23.5 percent tariff on aluminum products from China, Hong Kong, Russia, Venezuela and Vietnam, with a cap for all countries at 2017 import levels.\n\nAn aluminum import quota on all countries of 86.7 percent of their 2017 imports was also an option detailed in the report",
              "Trump could determine that specific countries should be exempted from the proposed quota, based on the economic or security interests of the United States. The president could also consider the countries' willingness to work with the United States to address global excess capacity and other challenges facing the U.S. aluminum and steel industries.\n\nCompanies can also seek appeals based on U.S. production of steel and aluminum and specific national security-based considerations",
              "Ross said each of the measures should enable U.S. steel producers to operate at 80 percent average capacity utilization rates or better based on available capacity in 2017.\n\nAccording to the Aluminum Association, there are five U.S. smelting facilities that are operating with three partial curtailments. This is a fraction compared with the 24 operational smelters up and running in 2001",
              "{\"headline\":\"Commerce Department calls for Trump to impose steep tariffs or quotas on foreign steel and aluminum\",\"dateCreated\":\"2018-02-16T16:53:29+0000\",\"datePublished\":\"2018-02-16T16:53:29+0000\",\"dateModified\":\"2018-02-16T23:30:44+0000\",\"author\":[{\"name\":\"Kayla Tausche\"},{\"name\":\"Lori Ann LaRocco\"},{\"name\":\"Tom DiChristopher\"}],\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"CNBC\",\"foundingDate\":\"1989-04-17\",\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/cnbc\",\"https://www.instagram.com/cnbc\",\"https://www.linkedin.com/company/cnbc\",\"https://twitter.com/cnbc\",\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNBC\",\"https://www.youtube.com/cnbc\"]},\"articleSection\":\"Business News\",\"keywords\":[\"cnbc\",\"Articles\",\"Donald J. Trump\",\"United States Steel Corp\",\"Investment strategy\",\"Politics\",\"Trade\",\"Stock markets\",\"stocks\",\"Investing\",\"Business News\",\"Economy\",\"source:tagname:CNBC US Source\"],\"video\":{\"description\":\"CNBC's Kayla Tausche reports on the Commerce Department set to unveil options to the White House on tariffs and import quotas.\",\"duration\":\"PT1M22S\",\"name\":\"Commerce to unveil options on quotas, tariffs\",\"uploadDate\":\"2018-02-16T16:24:54+0000\"}}"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/morning-trade/2018/02/20/us-trading-partners-brace-for-232-action-110802",
            "title": "U.S. trading partners brace for 232 action - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# U.S. trading partners brace for 232 action\n*With help from Doug Palmer and Hans von der Burchard*\n\n**U.S. TRADING PARTNERS BRACE FOR 232 ACTION:** The slate of recommendations Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross gave President Donald Trump to impose restrictions on steel and aluminum imports reverberated around the world over the weekend, with U.S. allies and trading partners in Asia and elsewhere warning the administration against taking drastic action",
              "**In Japan**, the government did not comment directly, noting that a final decision had not been reached. But Yasuji Komiyama, director of the metal industries division of Japan’s Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, told Reuters on Monday that “Japan believes any steel and aluminium imports by the U.S. from Japan do not pose any threat to the U.S. national security.” Japan Iron and Steel Federation Chairman Kosei Shindo added that Japanese industry hopes “Trump would make a careful and appropriate judgement.”",
              "The 232 reports Commerce released on Friday laid out a series of ways Trump could limit imports to protect U.S. national security, including by imposing blanket tariffs of 24 percent on all steel imports and 7.7 percent for aluminum",
              "**In South Korea**, officials were upset over one of the options Commerce outlined that would exclude a number of NATO allies and trading partners but hit Seoul and 11 other nations with tariffs and quotas. The South Korean trade ministry said Monday it would consider filing a World Trade Organization complaint if Trump chose that option, Korean media reported",
              "**BROWN’S OFFER TO TRUMP:** Closer to home, Sen. Sherrod Brown (D-Ohio) responded to the reports on Friday by inviting Trump to tour an Ohio steel mill.\n\n“This opportunity would allow you to see firsthand how impressive our steel manufacturers are and hear directly from the workers about the impact global steel overcapacity and unfair trade practices have had on their jobs and families,” Brown said in a letter to Trump",
              "**SCHUMER LIFTS HOLD ON KAPLAN:** One immediate consequence of Ross going public with the 232 reports was Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer’s decision to release his hold on Gilbert Kaplan’s nomination to be undersecretary of Commerce for international trade. Trump nominated Kaplan more than ten months ago. Schumer placed a hold on his nomination last fall to pressure Trump to follow through on Section 232 relief for the steel and aluminum sectors",
              "**He could soon be busy:** If confirmed, one of Kaplan’s first tasks would be managing domestic and international fall-out if Trump decides to restrict steel and aluminum imports under the rarely used national security justification. That would include industry requests for certain steel and aluminum products to be excluded from proposed tariffs and/or quotas",
              "Schumer’s decision to lift his hold “makes a lot of sense to us,” said Thomas Sneeringer, president of the Committee to Support U.S. Trade Laws, a group that advocates for the aggressive use of trade remedy laws. “Any sweeping relief program, such as recommended by Commerce, is going to require skillful and careful implementation to be as fair to all as possible and to avoid unintended consequences.”",
              "**LEADING BUSINESS GROUPS RAISE 232 CONCERNS:** If the Trump administration was hoping that by releasing its determination that national security is threatened by current volumes of steel and aluminum imports they would trigger a rally-around-the-flag effect … well, they’re still waiting. While some steel and aluminum groups praised the recommendations, many other segments of the business community expressed alarm about the course Ross suggested",
              "“Business Roundtable is concerned that acting on the Commerce Department’s recommendations to use Section 232 to restrict steel and aluminum imports will result in foreign retaliation against U.S. exporters and harm the U.S. economy,” the group said in a statement.\n\nThe American Automotive Policy Council, which represents Ford, General Motors and Chrysler-Fiat, urged Trump to fashion a solution that won’t “diminish the global competitiveness of America’s automotive industry” by leading American carmakers to pay higher prices for steel and aluminum",
              "“This would place the U.S. automotive industry, which supports more than 7 million American jobs, at a competitive disadvantage,” the group said.\n\n**Rufus Yerxa, the National Foreign Trade Council president and former deputy director of the World Trade Organization,** said he did not believe the use of national security to justify steel and aluminum import restrictions was legitimate, and feared it would lead to copycat moves by other countries to keep out U.S. exports",
              "**FOREIGN STEEL PRODUCERS PREDICT DAMAGING FALLOUT:** The American Institute for International Steel, which represents foreign steel producers, picked up on that theme and urged Trump to reject Ross’ recommendations, rather than “risk the nation’s well-being in order to benefit a few politically favored companies.”",
              "“The national security foundation for the recommended tariffs and quotas is simply an unfortunate attempt to circumvent normally applicable WTO rules,” AIIS Chairman John Foster said in a statement. “If the United States chooses to abandon long-standing principles of free trade that we have helped establish, and that have contributed so much to our national prosperity, Pandora’s box will be opened, and other countries will be sure to assert ‘national security’ reasons for protecting many other politically sensitive products from export competition.”",

⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]

              "> \"\\[T\\]he opening shot in a trade war... a declaration of war against the world on aluminum and steel... These are some of our closest treaty allies... These are some serious numbers.\"\n\nQuote from a trade expert:\n\n> \"This would be beyond a trade war. You're talking about blowing up the WTO.\""
            ]
          }
